# American English: broad transcription to narrow transcription.
#
# The classical statement of what this does. A broad (phonemic) reading
# writes one symbol per phoneme; a narrow (phonetic) reading writes the
# allophonic detail that is predictable from context. These rules are that
# prediction, so they take /pˈɪn/ to [pʰˈɪ̃n] and /bˈʌtɚ/ to [bˈʌɾɚ].
#
# Ordered. Each rule sees the previous rule's output, which is where
# feeding and bleeding live; within a rule every site is found against a
# snapshot, so no rule feeds itself.
#
# Stress is marked on the NUCLEUS, not at the syllable boundary, so a
# stressed vowel is a single unit and no syllabification is required. Use
# IPAFeatures.normalize_stress_to_nucleus if the input marks stress at the
# boundary; normalize_stress_to_syllable puts it back.
#
# A rule needing a syllable margin writes '.', which means "a syllable
# margin or stronger" -- the tiers nest, so a word edge is also a syllable
# margin, and the edge of the form counts without one being typed. A word
# written without interior dots leaves its interior margins UNSPECIFIED,
# and a margin-conditioned rule does not fire there rather than guessing.
# That is why aspiration reaches 'pˈɪn' and 'ə.tˈæk' but not 'ətˈæk'.
#
# THE STRESS MARK IN THAT SENTENCE IS LOAD-BEARING and was missing until
# a reader tried it. MEASURED: 'pɪn' -> [pɪ̃n], unaspirated, because the
# rule asks for '[vowel stress=primary]' and an unstressed transcription
# does not carry one; 'pˈɪn' -> [pʰˈɪ̃n]. The point the sentence makes is
# about the MARGIN and is right; the illustration had simply dropped the
# other condition the rule depends on.
#
# Aspiration is stated positively, as a margin condition, not as "not
# after /s/": in 'spin' the margin is occupied by /s/, so /p/ is not at
# one. That is both the standard generalization and the reason no negative
# context is needed. Classical SPE negates feature values, not context
# positions, and feature-value negation is available -- '[-voiced]'.
#
# CONSTITUENCY IS ASSIGNED BEFORE ANYTHING READS IT, which is why the
# syllabic-consonant block has moved UP the file, above the two rules that
# want to know what it decides. Neither can work it out from adjacency:
# what English flaps a /t/ before, and what a stop takes a lateral release
# into, is a NUCLEUS, not "an /l/". Run the block before them and
# '[syllabic=+]' is a question they can ask; run it after -- as this file
# did -- and each has to guess from the segments beside it, and guesses
# wrong wherever the segmental string and the syllable structure disagree.
# That divergence is the one shape of mistake this file has repeatedly
# made; the notes below mark each place it did.

# --- tapping ------------------------------------------------------------
# Both alveolar stops tap, not just /t/. Written as a class so /d/ is not
# a second rule saying the same thing.
#
# THE CONDITION IS ON THE FOLLOWING NUCLEUS, NOT THE PRECEDING ONE.
# Flapping is FOOT-MEDIAL; what closes a foot is the next stress, so the
# segmental shadow of "inside a foot" is the following vowel's lack of
# one. 'editor' is the argument in one word: /d/ and /t/ sit in the same
# word, only the /d/ follows the stress, and GA flaps both -- what the two
# share is what stands in FRONT of them.
#
# WHY THE PRECEDING-STRESS ANALYSIS IS ABANDONED rather than dismissed. It
# is a real if minority textbook position and it is right about a lot:
# every word a textbook cites first is a trochee, where the two statements
# pick out the same /t/, and it gets 'ə.tˈæk' right for free. What it
# cannot do is reach a flap in any foot after the first, and it has to add
# as a separate stipulation that /t/ before a stressed vowel never flaps.
# MEASURED on '[vowel stress=primary] _ [vowel]', which is what this file
# asked for until a review took it past a trochee:
#
#   butter    /bˈʌtɚ/     -> bˈʌɾɚ      right, and right by coincidence
#   editor    /ˈɛdɪtɚ/    -> ˈɛɾɪtɚ     GA has ˈɛɾɪɾɚ: the second /t/ too
#   capital   /kˈæpɪtəl/  -> kʰˈæpɪtəɫ  GA has kʰˈæpɪɾəɫ
#   positive  /pˈɑzɪtɪv/  -> pʰˈɑzɪtɪv  GA has pʰˈɑzɪɾɪv
#
# The following-vowel statement gets the second fact from the same
# condition as the first, and it is what the standard generalization says.
#
# 'UNSTRESSED' IS THE ABSENCE OF THE FEATURE, NOT A VALUE OF IT. 'stress'
# declares 'primary' and 'secondary' and NO DEFAULT -- MEASURED,
# f.features['stress'].default is None -- so there is no unmarked value to
# name. '∅' is how the absence is WRITTEN on the right of an arrow, and it
# is not a declared VALUE, so a CONTEXT cannot ask for it: '[vowel
# stress=∅]' beside the '_' is refused, while '[vowel] -> [stress=∅]' is
# the documented way to clear one. The negation has to be per value, and
# negating both values is exactly "carries no stress".
#
# AND NOT '[vowel -stress]', WHICH LOOKS LIKE IT SAYS THAT AND IS REFUSED.
# A '-' term resolves as a feature VALUE first and as a BINARY feature
# name second; 'stress' is neither, so the term names nothing and the
# query raises. MEASURED, '[vowel] _ [vowel -stress]' gives: "feature
# 'stress' is not binary, so there is no '-' value to take. Its declared
# values are ['primary', 'secondary']; negate them individually instead,
# as '-primary -secondary'". Which is what this file writes.
#
# SECONDARY STRESS BLOCKS IT TOO, which is what '-secondary' buys: a
# secondary-stressed syllable heads its own foot, so a flap before it
# would not be foot-medial. MEASURED, 'mˈɪlɪtˌɛɹi' keeps its /t/ and
# 'ˈæ.lə.ɡˌe͜ɪ.tɚ' flaps the one that has no stress in front of it. Some
# descriptions flap before a secondary too; this file takes the
# conservative reading and says so rather than leaving it to the default.
# Both spellings mark stress on the NUCLEUS, and that matters here more
# than anywhere else in the file: written at the boundary, 'ˈmɪlɪˌtɛɹi'
# hangs the secondary mark on the /t/ and leaves the 'ɛ' unstressed, so
# the rule flaps it. Run IPAFeatures.normalize_stress_to_nucleus first,
# as the head of this file says.
#
# NOT ORDERED BEFORE ASPIRATION, and the argument that it must be -- a
# stop which has become a tap must not then be read as a voiceless plosive
# and aspirated -- does not hold under this statement. The two rules
# CANNOT compete: tapping wants an unstressed nucleus to the right and
# aspiration a primary-stressed one, so no segment satisfies both.
# MEASURED, swapping them changes no derivation in the corpus, and so does
# lifting all three tapping rules into one block below the syllabic block.
# The split is presentation -- these two open the file because they are
# the two rules a reader learns the notation from -- and the one real
# ordering constraint is the syllabic block's, stated below.
[manner=plosive place=alveolar] -> [manner=tap voiced=+] / [vowel] _ [vowel -primary -secondary] ; tapping

# --- aspiration ---------------------------------------------------------
[manner=plosive voiced=-] -> [release=aspirated] / . _ [vowel stress=primary] ; aspiration

# --- devoicing of an approximant after a voiceless stop -----------------
# 'pray' [pɹ̥eɪ], 'clean' [kl̥in], 'twin' [tw̥ɪn], 'cute' [kj̥ut].
# The following vowel matters: this is an ONSET CLUSTER process. Without
# it the rule also devoices a word-final syllabic lateral -- MEASURED,
# drop the '[vowel]' and 'bˈɑtl' comes out [bˈɑɾl̥̩], 'ˈæpl' [ˈæpˡl̥̩] --
# and the /l/ there is the nucleus, not the second half of a cluster with
# the stop.
[manner=approximant voiced=+] -> [phonation=devoiced] / [manner=plosive voiced=-] _ [vowel] ; approximant devoicing

# --- nasal place assimilation -------------------------------------------
# ONE RULE, and it used to be two. This is SPE's agreement variable
# (docs/rules.md): 'α' binds to whatever place the following consonant
# carries, and the change writes that same place onto the /n/. The
# enumeration it replaces was
#
#     n -> [place=bilabial] / _ [place=bilabial] ; nasal assimilation (labial)
#     n -> [place=velar]    / _ [place=velar]    ; nasal assimilation (velar)
#
# and the note that stood here said the general statement was not
# expressible. It is now, and the two lines were never the phonology:
# English assimilates to every place a following consonant offers, and the
# enumeration reached the two that a corpus of 'input' and 'income'
# happens to need.
#
# THE COLLAPSE IS NOT BEHAVIOR-PRESERVING, and that is the point rather
# than a cost. MEASURED, over 'n' before each of the 139 phones that spell
# themselves back, in three frames -- 417 forms, 96 differ, and every one
# of them is at a place the two rules never named: labiodental, dental,
# palatal, uvular, bilabial^velar. At bilabial and velar the two spellings
# agree exactly; at alveolar the change is a no-op, because /n/ is already
# there and a rule that changes nothing does not fire. Over this set's own
# corpus, before the two words below were added to it, every derivation
# was byte-identical.
#
# The places it now reaches are the ones a description of English lists:
# 'infant' [ˈɪ̃ɱfə̃nt] before /f/, 'tenth' [tʰˈɛ̃n̪θ] before /θ/. Both are in
# the corpus as pins, so the widening stays visible rather than waiting to
# be discovered.
#
# THE VARIABLE BINDS ON THE LEFT OF THE ARROW AND IS WRITTEN ON THE RIGHT.
# A place has to be somewhere for '[place=α]' to bind to, so /n/ before a
# VOWEL does not assimilate: no vowel declares a place, so the context
# does not hold. That is the same reading under which '[place=alveolar]'
# does not match a vowel, and not a special case for variables.
n -> [place=α] / _ [place=α] ; nasal assimilation

# --- syllabic consonants ------------------------------------------------
# 'button' [bʌtn̩], 'bottle' [bɑtl̩]: a nasal or lateral carries the
# syllable when it follows a consonant at the end of a word.
#
# MOVED UP, and that is the ordering change this file's fixes rest on. It
# used to sit below the release rules, which want to know what it decides;
# see the release block for what that cost. Nothing here reads anything
# the rules above produced -- the context is an obstruent and a word end,
# and no rule in this set creates or destroys either -- so the block could
# stand anywhere before its readers, and MEASURED, lifting it and the two
# rules that read it to the top of the file, together, changes no
# derivation in the corpus. It sits here, after aspiration, so that the
# two rules a reader learns the notation from still open the file.
#
# What IS load-bearing is the block standing before its readers, and both
# halves are measured rather than argued: put 'tapping (before a syllabic
# lateral)' above it and 'bˈɑtl' comes out [bˈɑtˡl̩] with no flap, put
# 'lateral release' above it and 'ˈæpl' comes out [ˈæpl̩] with no release.
#
# AFTER AN OBSTRUENT, not after any consonant. '[-vowel] _ #' was too wide
# by exactly the sonorants, and the over-application is audible: MEASURED,
# it made 'ˈɑɹm' [ˈɑɹm̩], 'fˈɪlm' [fˈɪɫm̩] and 'kˈɪln' [kʰˈɪɫn̩], all of
# which are ONE syllable in English, while getting 'pɹˈɪzm' [pɹ̥ˈɪzm̩] right
# for the reason it gets 'button' right -- the nasal is behind an
# obstruent. A sonorant and the sonorant behind it share a rhyme; a
# sonorant behind an obstruent has to be a nucleus, because English has no
# obstruent+sonorant coda.
#
# 'obstruent' is a declared natural class -- ipa.xml puts it on three
# values of 'manner' -- so the rule names it, and this file and the
# shipped german-final-devoicing set read the same declaration rather than
# each spelling the class out. Both used to write it as its definition,
# '[-vowel -approximant -nasal -trill -tap -silence]', because the
# resolver routed values and not class names.
[manner=nasal] -> [syllabic=+] / [obstruent] _ # ; syllabic nasal
# The lateral's class is the obstruents PLUS THE NASALS, and the extra
# manner is not a slip. It stays spelled by exclusion because a bracket is
# a conjunction: '[obstruent]' already excludes the nasals and no term can
# put them back, so this union has no name in the declaration and is
# written as its definition instead.
# 'ˈfa͜ɪnl' is [ˈfa͜ɪnl̩] and 'ˈt͡ʃænl' [ˈt͡ʃæ̃nl̩] --
# final, channel, tunnel, colonel: an /l/ behind a nasal is a nucleus,
# where an /l/ behind a rhotic is not ('snˈɑɹl' is one syllable). So the
# two rules do not share a class, and writing them with one would be
# tidier than the language is.
#
# A class, not the literal 'l'. Written as a literal it was bled by the
# devoicing rule above: while that rule had no '[vowel]' in its context,
# 'ˈbɑtl' had already become 'ˈbɑtl̥', which a literal no longer matches, so
# 'bottle' came out non-syllabic while its nasal twin -- written as a class
# -- fired correctly. The asymmetry hid the defect. Both halves are fixed
# now and the class stays, because it is what the rule means.
[channel=lateral manner=approximant] -> [syllabic=+] / [-vowel -approximant -trill -tap -silence] _ # ; syllabic lateral

# --- tapping, continued -------------------------------------------------
# THREE RULES IN ALL, because "between a nucleus and a following
# UNSTRESSED one" is not a statement about adjacent VOWELS, and every
# place it is written as one it is wrong at a different edge. The three
# share a right-hand condition -- the next nucleus carries no stress --
# and differ only in what the notation has to write to reach a nucleus
# that is not spelled as a vowel, or a rhyme that does not end in one. A
# feature query is a conjunction, so "a vowel OR a syllabic lateral"
# cannot be said once and the cases are spelled out.
#
# That USED to be "the same limit the nasal-assimilation block records",
# and it is not: the nasal block states its generalization in one line
# now, with an agreement variable. This one still cannot, and the reason
# is a different one worth being exact about -- a variable says "the same
# value here and there", which is agreement, and what these three rules
# want is a DISJUNCTION over two ways of being a nucleus. Nothing about
# the value is shared between them.
#
# These two stand here, below aspiration, rather than beside the rule they
# extend, because the first of them has to see the syllabic block's work.
# That dependency is the only reason left: as the tapping block above now
# records, no tapping rule can compete with aspiration for a segment, and
# MEASURED, lifting all three into one block here changes no derivation in
# the corpus. The two rules that open the file open it for a reader, not
# for the cascade.
#
# On the RIGHT the following nucleus may be a syllabic consonant: 'bottle'
# is [ˈbɑɾl̩], flapped, and MEASURED this file gave [bˈɑtl̩] with a plain
# /t/ for as long as tapping asked for '[vowel]' there. The repair is not
# a wider vowel class -- a syllabic /l/ is not a vowel by any feature it
# carries -- it is to run the syllabic block first and then ASK, which is
# what '[syllabic=+ channel=lateral]' does, and why the block above moved.
# It carries the same '-primary -secondary' as the other two, because a
# nucleus is a nucleus: nothing in this file's corpus stresses a syllabic
# lateral, but the rule states the generalization rather than relying on
# nobody typing one.
#
# ONLY THE LATERAL. A syllabic NASAL takes a glottal stop in GA ('button'
# [ˈbʌʔn̩]), which is a rule this set does not write, and flapping into it
# would be a second wrong answer rather than a repair.
[manner=plosive place=alveolar] -> [manner=tap voiced=+] / [vowel] _ [syllabic=+ channel=lateral -primary -secondary] ; tapping (before a syllabic lateral)
# On the LEFT the nucleus may be followed by a coda /ɹ/ that is part of
# the same rhyme: 'party' is [pʰˈɑɹɾi]. Whether tapping fires used to
# depend on how the rhotic was TRANSCRIBED, which is a property of the
# typist and not of the language -- 'bˈʌtɚ' flaps because 'ɚ' is a vowel,
# 'pˈɑɹti' did not because 'ɹ' is an approximant, and the two spell the
# same rhyme. Naming '/ɹ/' and not '[-vowel]' is deliberate: 'salty' and
# 'shelter' do not flap after /l/, so this is the rhotic's fact and not a
# coda's. The left context is a vowel and no longer a STRESSED vowel, for
# the reason the block above gives; what the '[vowel]' is still doing here
# is requiring the /t/ to be inside a rhyme at all, which is what keeps
# word-initial /t/ out -- 'təmˈe͜ɪto͜ʊ' has an unstressed vowel to the
# right of its first /t/ and must not flap it.
[manner=plosive place=alveolar] -> [manner=tap voiced=+] / [vowel] ɹ _ [vowel -primary -secondary] ; tapping (after a coda rhotic)

# --- release phases -----------------------------------------------------
# A stop released into a homorganic nasal or a lateral releases that way
# rather than orally.
[manner=plosive] -> [release=nasal] / _ [manner=nasal] ; nasal release
# THE TWO ARE NOT SYMMETRICAL, and the asymmetry is English phonotactics
# rather than an oversight. A nasal after a stop can only be a nucleus or
# a following syllable's onset -- there is no /pn kn tm/ ONSET to protect
# -- so the nasal rule can be stated over the manner and left alone. An
# /l/ after a stop very often is an onset: 'plʌs' and 'klˈin' are /pl/ and
# /kl/ clusters, and a lateral release there is not what the rule means.
#
# So the lateral rule asks for a NUCLEUS. Written with the literal 'l' it
# was bled the way this file's own note above describes -- MEASURED, the
# syllabic rule had already made it 'l̩', which the literal no longer
# matched, so 'bˈɑtl' came out [bˈɑtl̩] with no lateral release at all
# while 'bˈʌtn', whose rule is a class, got [bˈʌtⁿn̩]. That is the SAME
# literal-versus-class defect the syllabic block records, surviving one
# block further down in the file that warns about it. Widening it to the
# lateral class alone would then have caught the onsets and given
# [pˡl̥ʌs]; '[syllabic=+ channel=lateral]' is the condition that means what
# the sentence at the top of this block says. 'ˈæpl' -> [ˈæpˡl̩], and
# 'bˈɑtl' -> [bˈɑɾl̩] because tapping has already taken the /t/, which is
# what GA does with it.
[manner=plosive] -> [release=lateral] / _ [syllabic=+ channel=lateral] ; lateral release

# --- unreleased coda ----------------------------------------------------
# Word-final voiceless stops are typically unreleased.
[manner=plosive voiced=-] -> [release=no-audible] / _ # ; unreleased coda

# --- dark l -------------------------------------------------------------
# Registered as ɫ, so respell answers before composition is reached.
l -> [velarized=+] / [vowel] _ ; dark l

# --- vowel nasalization -------------------------------------------------
# A CODA nasal, not any nasal to the right. English nasalizes a vowel
# before a nasal IN ITS OWN SYLLABLE; a nasal that is the next syllable's
# onset leaves it alone. MEASURED, the one-rule spelling '_ [manner=nasal]'
# nasalized 'ˈmʌni' to [ˈmʌ̃ni], 'ˈsʌmɚ' to [ˈsʌ̃mɚ] and 'təmˈe͜ɪto͜ʊ' to
# [tə̃mˈe͜ɪɾo͜ʊ] -- three words where the nasal is an onset and the vowel is
# oral. This is the same divergence as tapping's, from the other side:
# adjacency says yes and constituency says no.
#
# TWO RULES, because what is wanted is "a nasal that is not followed by a
# vowel" -- it has a consonant after it, or the word ends -- and position
# negation is not expressible (docs/rules.md says so in as many words).
# '_ [manner=nasal] [-vowel]' is the closed-syllable case and
# '_ [manner=nasal] #' the word-final one, and '#' fires at the end of a
# form with nothing typed, which is what makes 'pˈɪn' work.
#
# It is an APPROXIMATION and worth knowing as one: "not before a vowel" is
# where the syllable breaks in every English word in this file's corpus,
# but it is a segmental test standing in for a structural one, and a
# transcription that marks its syllables is not consulted. Naming '.'
# instead -- '_ [manner=nasal] .' -- would consult it, and would then give
# one answer for 'hˈæn.dbˌæɡ' and another for 'hˈændbˌæɡ', which is the
# optional-notation failure this repo has fixed twice. The segmental test
# is the one that treats the two spellings alike.
#
# The word-final rule keeps the unqualified name because it is the one
# every worked example in this repo shows -- 'pˈɪn' [pʰˈɪ̃n] -- and a trace
# that renamed it would leave those accounts naming a rule that no longer
# exists.
[vowel] -> [nasalized=+] / _ [manner=nasal] # ; nasalization
[vowel] -> [nasalized=+] / _ [manner=nasal] [-vowel] ; nasalization (closed syllable)

# --- a note on diphthongs, worth knowing before debugging a rule --------
# Whether a diphthong is TIED changes what a rule sees, because untied
# 'eɪ' is two units and a stress mark lands on the first of them. The
# tied spelling is the one this library treats as a single vocalic unit
# (docs/ties.md), so tie diphthongs in the input if allophony should see
# them whole.
#
# TAPPING IS NOT AN EXAMPLE OF THIS, because it asks nothing about stress
# on its left. MEASURED over 119700 tied/untied pairs of the shape
# C?VCVC?, built from five diphthongs and every stress placement: 18900
# derive differently, and NOT ONE of them differs in the number of taps.
# 'pə.tˈeɪ.toʊ' and 'pə.tˈe͜ɪ.to͜ʊ' both flap. That is one fewer place where
# a transcriber's spelling decides a phonological question, which is the
# complaint the coda-rhotic rule above was written to fix.
#
# WHERE THE TIE IS STILL LOAD-BEARING: nasalization, and in that same
# sweep it is the ONLY place -- all 18900 differences are the nasal mark
# and nothing else. MEASURED, 'ˈkaɪn' nasalizes its second element to
# [ˈkaɪ̃n] where 'ˈka͜ɪn' is left alone, because the rule reaches one unit
# and the composed mark on a tied pair does not read back. So do not
# conclude from the paragraph above that ties may be dropped.
#
# TIE THEM BY HAND. This file used to say "ipakit.add_ties() does it for
# registered pairs", and that is false: MEASURED, add_ties ties EVERY
# adjacent pair of base phones -- add_ties('kæt') is 'k͡æ͡t' -- so applied
# to the word above it gives 'p͡ə.tʰˈe͜ɪ.t͡o͜ʊ', where tapping does not fire
# at all. Its own docstring is accurate and says "within a multi-phone
# SEGMENT"; it is a segment tool, and it was this file that mis-sold it as
# a word tool. docs/rules.md and japanese-moraic.rules both warn about it,
# which is how the claim survived here for as long as it did: the warning
# was somewhere else.

# --- recorded rather than fixed, and deliberately not modeled ----------
# Named so the gaps are known rather than discovered. This set shipped
# without such a section, which is part of how it came to have four
# over-applications in it: nothing in the file distinguished "this is
# right" from "nobody has looked".
#
# * ASPIRATION UNDER-APPLIES WORD-INITIALLY BEFORE AN UNSTRESSED VOWEL.
#   GA aspirates a voiceless stop at the start of a word whether or not
#   the syllable is stressed -- 'tomato' is [tʰəˈme͜ɪɾo͜ʊ], 'potato'
#   [pʰəˈte͜ɪɾo͜ʊ] -- and the rule above asks for a following
#   '[vowel stress=primary]', so it declines. MEASURED: 'təmˈe͜ɪto͜ʊ' comes
#   out with a plain /t/. Nothing about the notation is in the way. The
#   rule is one line,
#
#       [manner=plosive voiced=-] -> [release=aspirated] / # _ [vowel]
#
#   and it is correct: '#' does not reach the /p/ of 'spin', which is
#   behind an /s/, so the positive margin statement above is untouched.
#   It is left out here for a reason that is not phonological and is worth
#   stating plainly rather than leaving as a silent gap: it aspirates the
#   first /p/ of 'pə.tˈe͜ɪ.to͜ʊ', and that derivation is quoted as worked
#   output in docs/rules.md and replayed line for line by
#   tests/test_cli.py. The rule and that document have to move in one
#   commit, so it is left for the commit that can move both.
# * GLOTTALISATION. GA has [ˈbʌʔn̩] for 'button', not the [ˈbʌtⁿn̩] this
#   set derives, and /t/ is glottalized before a syllabic nasal and often
#   word-finally. The nasal-release answer is a defensible narrow reading
#   of a more conservative register; a set that wrote the glottal rule
#   would have to order it before tapping and before nasal release, and
#   would then owe an account of which of the two this file's 'bˈʌtn'
#   examples mean.
# * The rhotic's other spellings. Tapping now reaches a coda '/ɹ/' and the
#   r-colored vowels, which is every way this set's corpus writes the
#   rhyme of 'party'. What cannot be said once is the generalization
#   underneath -- "after a rhyme" -- because a rhyme is a constituent and a
#   context is a sequence of units; each of its endings is a separate line,
#   and a spelling nobody enumerated is a spelling that does not flap.
# * THE FOOT IS NOT MODELLED, only its segmental shadow. "Foot-medial" is
#   what tapping means and "the next nucleus carries no stress" is what it
#   asks, and the two come apart wherever a foot boundary is not marked by
#   a stress: across a word boundary inside a phrase ('hit it' flaps, 'hit
#   Tim' does not, and this set sees no phrase), and in any transcription
#   that leaves an unstressed monosyllable unmarked. That is the same
#   shape of approximation the nasalization block records from the other
#   side -- a segmental test standing in for a structural one -- and it is
#   recorded here for the same reason: so it is known rather than
#   discovered.
